/*
  core/stressors/cognitive_load.spthy -- f_U for sigma1 HighCognitiveLoad, CEREMONY-AGNOSTIC.

  Migrated to the agnostic interface (AGNOSTIC_STRESSOR_INTERFACE.md). sigma1 used to exist in TWO
  files keyed on ceremony specifics: this one matched a designer flag !Req(P,_,'CALC_SK',_,'Hard'),
  and cognitive_load_inferred.spthy (now deleted) matched the objective op !Op(P,_,'kdf',_). Both
  encoded the SAME judgment -- "this step is mentally hard" -- in the detector. That judgment now
  lives ONCE in the lexicon, so the two collapse into this single detector, which reads:

    * !Step(P, sid, action)                -- Layer 1: an OBJECTIVE interaction step (no task name)
    * !Demands(action,'MentalDemand','hi') -- Layer 2: the usability verdict, from core/lexicon_tlx

  A 'compute' step (KDF, passphrase setup) is high mental demand per the lexicon, so this fires for
  both ceremonies with no 'CALC_SK'/'kdf'/'Hard' token in the rule.

  NASA-TLX Mental Demand (Hart & Staveland 1988) / cognitive load theory (Sweller 1988). At onset it
  emits SetMask(P,'Busy') -- everything below SetMask is UNCHANGED. !StressEnable(P) gates per party
  (Stage B); OneStressPerParty bounds the onset to one per party.
*/

rule Trigger_HighCognitiveLoad:
    [ !Step(P, sid, action),
      !Demands(action, 'MentalDemand', 'hi'),
      !StressEnable(P) ]
  --[ Load(P), SetMask(P,'Busy') ]->
    [ ]

restriction OneStressPerParty:
  "All p #i #j. Load(p)@i & Load(p)@j ==> #i = #j"
